/*!
    \page style-checkbox-walkthrough.html
    \title Styling a Checkbox: A Walkthrough
    \brief How a QCheckBox is drawn, from its paint event through QCommonStyle
    to a custom indicator in a proxy style.

    This page follows a checkbox from the moment it receives a paint event to
    the moment the style finishes painting. It shows how the widget builds its
    style option, how \l QCommonStyle breaks the element into parts, and how a
    \l QProxyStyle replaces one of those parts. Most widgets follow the same
    structure, so the same reading strategy works for them: find the style
    option the widget builds, find the elements it draws, and read the
    \l QCommonStyle implementation of those elements.

    \section1 The widget builds a style option

    \l QCheckBox uses \l QStyleOptionButton. Its \c initStyleOption() function
    fills the option out like this, slightly simplified:

    \snippet code/doc_src_styles.cpp 0

    \l QStyleOption::initFrom() sets the information that every widget shares.
    Its implementation amounts to this:

    \snippet code/doc_src_styles.cpp 1

    \c State_Enabled is set when the widget is enabled, \c State_HasFocus when
    it has focus, \c State_KeyboardFocusChange when the user last changed focus
    with the keyboard, and \c State_Active when the widget's window is the
    active window. \c State_MouseOver is set while the mouse cursor is over the
    widget. In addition to the state, \c initFrom() stores the layout direction,
    the widget's rectangle, its palette, and its font metrics in the option.

    \l QCheckBox then adds its own state. \c State_Sunken is set while the user
    presses the box, whether it's checked or not. \c State_NoChange is set for a
    partially checked tristate box; otherwise \c State_On or \c State_Off
    reflects the check state. The option also carries the text, the icon, and
    the icon size. \l QCheckBox keeps \c State_MouseOver only while the widget
    has the \l{Qt::}{WA_Hover} attribute, which a style typically sets in
    \l{QStyle::}{polish()}.

    Suppose the user presses the checked checkbox in the following screenshot
    with the mouse while it has focus. The screenshot also shows a radio button,
    which uses the same structure with \c PE_IndicatorRadioButton and
    \c CE_RadioButtonLabel:

    \image styles/checkbox.webp
           {Checkbox and radio button with their indicator, label, and focus
           rectangles outlined and named in a legend}

    At that moment, its option has these state flags:

    \table 90%
    \header
        \li State flag
        \li Set
    \row
        \li \c State_Sunken
        \li Yes
    \row
        \li \c State_NoChange
        \li No
    \row
        \li \c State_On
        \li Yes
    \row
        \li \c State_Off
        \li No
    \row
        \li \c State_MouseOver
        \li Yes
    \row
        \li \c State_Enabled
        \li Yes
    \row
        \li \c State_HasFocus
        \li Yes
    \row
        \li \c State_KeyboardFocusChange
        \li No
    \row
        \li \c State_Active
        \li Yes
    \endtable

    \section1 The widget asks the style to draw

    \l QCheckBox paints itself in \l{QWidget::}{paintEvent()} with a
    \l QStylePainter, which wraps the drawing functions of \l QStyle:

    \snippet code/doc_src_styles.cpp 2

    That's all the widget does. Everything else happens in the style.

    \section1 QCommonStyle breaks the element into parts

    \l QCommonStyle handles \c CE_CheckBox by asking for the rectangles of its
    two subelements, \c SE_CheckBoxIndicator and \c SE_CheckBoxContents, and
    drawing an element into each. If the checkbox has focus, it also draws the
    focus frame:

    \snippet code/doc_src_styles.cpp 3

    Note the \c proxy() calls. A style always draws its child elements through
    \l{QStyle::}{proxy()} so that a \l QProxyStyle wrapped around it gets the
    chance to override them.

    \c CE_CheckBoxLabel is also implemented in \l QCommonStyle:

    \snippet code/doc_src_styles.cpp 4

    \l{QStyle::}{visualAlignment()} adjusts the alignment for the layout
    direction. The style draws the icon, if there is one, and shrinks the
    remaining text rectangle accordingly. \l{QStyle::}{drawItemText()} draws the
    text with the alignment, the layout direction, and the mnemonic taken into
    account, and it uses the palette to pick the text color. Drawing a label
    involves many details, and the base class handles them well, so a custom
    style rarely needs to reimplement it.

    \section1 A proxy style replaces the indicator

    The indicator, \c PE_IndicatorCheckBox, is where styles differ, so it's the
    part to replace. The following \l QProxyStyle subclass draws a rounded
    indicator from the palette colors in the option and forwards every other
    element to the base style:

    \snippet customstyle/checkboxstyle.cpp 0

    \snippet customstyle/checkboxstyle.cpp 1

    The implementation reads all of its information from the option. It picks
    the color group from \c State_Enabled and \c State_Active, highlights the
    frame while \c State_MouseOver is set, darkens the background while
    \c State_Sunken is set, and draws a check mark for \c State_On or a filled
    square for \c State_NoChange. Because every color comes from the palette,
    the indicator follows the application palette and works in both light and
    dark color schemes. It also saves and restores the painter, so the base
    class finds the painter in the state it expects.

    The same indicator appears wherever the style draws \c PE_IndicatorCheckBox:
    in checkboxes, in checkable group boxes, and in item views that use it for
    check marks. That reuse is the reason primitive elements exist.

    To use the style, install it before the application creates its widgets:

    \snippet customstyle/checkboxstyle.cpp 2

    \section1 Apply the same reading to other widgets

    To learn how a widget is drawn, you don't have to read all of the code. It's
    usually enough to know which style elements the widget draws, which states
    it sets, and what its style option contains. The widget builds an option and
    calls the style one or more times; the style draws the elements the widget
    asks for. \l{Widget Style Reference} lists exactly that for each widget.

    \sa {How a Style Draws a Widget}, {Widget Style Reference}, QProxyStyle,
        QStyleOptionButton
*/
